1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
A Humanitarians AI essay
I read this week has

2
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:04,650
a sentence I couldn't stop thinking about.

3
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:06,120
It's about Howard Gardner,
the psychologist

4
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:08,905
who split the mind into
separate intelligences.

5
00:00:08,966 --> 00:00:11,065
It says he never had to ask which
of them a machine might take,

6
00:00:11,105 --> 00:00:15,693
because in 1983, technology "was not yet
a serious competitor to any of them."

7
00:00:16,029 --> 00:00:19,180
So I went and read what Gardner
wrote after 1983. He did ask.

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,788
And he answered, one
intelligence at a time.

9
00:00:22,425 --> 00:00:25,693
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

10
00:00:26,118 --> 00:00:29,044
This video is about Gardner's
theory of multiple intelligences,

11
00:00:29,084 --> 00:00:32,303
and which of them, by Gardner's own
account, a machine has now walked into.

12
00:00:32,343 --> 00:00:34,346
Think of his theory as a
house with eight rooms.

13
00:00:34,386 --> 00:00:36,525
We'll go through it room by room,
and a light only comes on

14
00:00:36,545 --> 00:00:39,523
when Gardner himself says a
machine does what that room is for.

15
00:00:39,563 --> 00:00:40,764
By the end you'll know the count,

16
00:00:40,804 --> 00:00:44,185
and why the last two rooms are
the ones worth arguing about.

17
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:47,813
The house was built in 1983,
in a book called Frames of Mind.

18
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,145
Seven rooms at first:
linguistic, logical-mathematical,

19
00:00:51,165 --> 00:00:53,085
spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic,

20
00:00:53,105 --> 00:00:57,543
and two personal ones, understanding
other people and understanding yourself.

21
00:00:57,583 --> 00:01:00,332
In the mid-1990s he added
an eighth: naturalist,

22
00:01:00,372 --> 00:01:03,372
the knack for telling kinds
of living things apart.

23
00:01:03,525 --> 00:01:06,458
One honest note about the
architecture before we go in.

24
00:01:06,498 --> 00:01:08,585
Most of academic psychology
would draw this house

25
00:01:08,605 --> 00:01:11,723
with one big room, a single
general ability called g.

26
00:01:11,949 --> 00:01:14,325
A handbook chapter Gardner
co-wrote admits as much:

27
00:01:14,345 --> 00:01:17,385
his theory "would seem to put
MI theory at odds with g."

28
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:20,003
It also notes how few studies
were built to test it as a whole.

29
00:01:20,043 --> 00:01:23,667
So this is Gardner's floor plan,
not settled science. It's his house.

30
00:01:23,707 --> 00:01:26,119
We're asking what he says walked into it.

31
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:31,072
In 2019, Gardner said we'd built machines
that "equal or surpass human capacities,"

32
00:01:31,112 --> 00:01:32,524
but he didn't say which.

33
00:01:32,803 --> 00:01:35,516
Then, in October 2024,
he and two colleagues,

34
00:01:35,638 --> 00:01:39,403
Shinri Furuzawa and Annie Stachura,
walked the house themselves.

35
00:01:40,083 --> 00:01:43,895
Room one: words. Their verdict on
large language models is blunt.

36
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:48,347
They "clearly exhibit the signs" of
high linguistic intelligence. Light on.

37
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:50,572
Room two: logic and numbers.

38
00:01:50,755 --> 00:01:53,803
Same sentence, same verdict.
Light on. But here's the thing.

39
00:01:53,843 --> 00:01:56,466
In that same paper, the authors
tell the history of the field.

40
00:01:56,506 --> 00:01:58,965
Programs at the 1956
Dartmouth meeting, they write,

41
00:01:58,985 --> 00:02:02,185
already "carried out mathematical
and logical operations which,

42
00:02:02,205 --> 00:02:05,690
if carried out by human beings,
might have been considered intelligent."

43
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:08,670
So this light may have been on
since before the house was built.

44
00:02:08,710 --> 00:02:11,757
What was "by no means clear"
back then was conversation,

45
00:02:11,797 --> 00:02:13,097
and games of strategy.

46
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:16,075
The essay's sentence
needs that one correction.

47
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:19,074
Room three: space. And
this one surprised me.

48
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:22,247
Chess and Go are filed here,
under spatial, not logic.

49
00:02:22,287 --> 00:02:23,823
"Mastering games like chess or Go,"

50
00:02:23,843 --> 00:02:27,289
and robots "remembering and
navigating complex terrains."

51
00:02:27,714 --> 00:02:28,298
Light on.

52
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:30,310
Room four: music.

53
00:02:30,330 --> 00:02:34,342
Recognizing pieces, "creating credible
new ones in distinctive styles,"

54
00:02:34,382 --> 00:02:36,617
performing "with appropriate nuances."

55
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:37,345
Light on.

56
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:41,182
Room five is the one I'd have
bet stayed dark. The body.

57
00:02:41,222 --> 00:02:44,650
But properly built and trained, they
write, programs can play instruments,

58
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:47,561
pick up and "adroitly" handle
objects of different shapes,

59
00:02:47,601 --> 00:02:50,365
even compete "successfully
in athletic events."

60
00:02:50,553 --> 00:02:51,136
Light on.

61
00:02:51,917 --> 00:02:54,333
Room six, the one he
added later: naturalist.

62
00:02:54,373 --> 00:02:55,917
Recognizing and grouping living things,

63
00:02:55,937 --> 00:02:58,878
and made things too,
"from thimbles to airplanes."

64
00:02:58,952 --> 00:03:01,059
Light on. That's six rooms out of eight.

65
00:03:01,099 --> 00:03:03,977
And the paper's next line
is almost cheerful: "So far,

66
00:03:03,997 --> 00:03:07,173
computational systems emerge
as multiply intelligent!"

67
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:09,810
But hold on. Before the last two doors,

68
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:12,609
I went back to check how a
room was supposed to be earned.

69
00:03:12,649 --> 00:03:15,943
In 1983, a capacity had to
meet eight criteria to count.

70
00:03:16,119 --> 00:03:18,830
Four of them are about biology:
brains, childhoods, evolution,

71
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:23,085
and people with unusual minds, like
prodigies, savants, stroke patients.

72
00:03:23,529 --> 00:03:27,643
As I read it, you can't walk a machine
through half of that inspection at all.

73
00:03:27,683 --> 00:03:31,890
So for animals, plants and machines, the
2024 paper uses a different inspection:

74
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:32,813
six "symptoms."

75
00:03:33,275 --> 00:03:35,523
Solving a problem, making
a product, communicating,

76
00:03:35,543 --> 00:03:38,290
improving with practice,
teaching others, and awareness.

77
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:40,131
Five of those six you can watch happen.

78
00:03:40,171 --> 00:03:42,902
To me, that means the six lights
came on under a performance test,

79
00:03:42,942 --> 00:03:44,603
not the one the house was built with.

80
00:03:44,643 --> 00:03:47,643
And even then, nobody scored
it symptom by symptom.

81
00:03:47,866 --> 00:03:51,455
The paper just says these systems
show "a reasonable sample."

82
00:03:52,275 --> 00:03:54,244
Room seven: understanding other people.

83
00:03:54,284 --> 00:03:56,291
And here the paper
actually lists evidence.

84
00:03:56,331 --> 00:04:00,640
Programs doing diplomacy, salesmanship,
gamesmanship, therapeutic interactions.

85
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:02,955
On the new inspection, as I
read it, that looks like a pass.

86
00:04:02,975 --> 00:04:05,103
And still: "one should be more cautious."

87
00:04:05,143 --> 00:04:08,575
So the door's open a crack, and the light
stays off. What I can't tell is why.

88
00:04:08,595 --> 00:04:12,118
Is it waiting on a better test? Or is it
waiting on something no test could show?

89
00:04:12,158 --> 00:04:13,452
The paper doesn't say.

90
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:17,206
Room eight: understanding yourself.
This door they don't try to open.

91
00:04:17,246 --> 00:04:18,730
Using that word for a program at all,

92
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:21,341
the authors write, "can be
seen as a 'category error.'"

93
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:25,130
Machines, they note, "can be trained
to testify to or feign consciousness."

94
00:04:25,170 --> 00:04:28,348
So this room isn't dark because
a machine failed the inspection.

95
00:04:28,388 --> 00:04:32,252
It's dark because, on their reading,
the inspection doesn't apply.

96
00:04:32,292 --> 00:04:33,932
This February, Gardner stepped back from

97
00:04:33,952 --> 00:04:36,292
the rooms altogether:
"Our social, ethical,

98
00:04:36,312 --> 00:04:40,406
and moral lives cannot and should not
be consigned to any artificial entity."

99
00:04:40,446 --> 00:04:41,759
Listen to those two words.

100
00:04:41,799 --> 00:04:44,149
Cannot is about the machine.
Should not is about us.

101
00:04:44,189 --> 00:04:46,039
The first might be checkable, one day.

102
00:04:46,079 --> 00:04:48,032
The second isn't something
any test could settle.

103
00:04:48,052 --> 00:04:50,875
It's not an inspection.
It's who holds the keys.

104
00:04:51,383 --> 00:04:52,812
Which brings me back to that essay.

105
00:04:52,832 --> 00:04:55,538
It called AI "not
obsolescence, its opposite."

106
00:04:55,814 --> 00:04:58,163
Its picture is a forklift:
the smart response isn't

107
00:04:58,183 --> 00:05:01,476
to practise lifting heavier things,
it's to learn to run the machine.

108
00:05:01,516 --> 00:05:03,809
This August, Gardner
reached for a third word.

109
00:05:03,849 --> 00:05:05,003
He was talking about a different list,

110
00:05:05,023 --> 00:05:07,973
from his 2007 book Five
Minds for the Future.

111
00:05:08,436 --> 00:05:11,848
The disciplined, synthesizing
and creating minds, he said,

112
00:05:11,892 --> 00:05:14,023
will be handled by AI
"so well that pursuing

113
00:05:14,043 --> 00:05:16,573
them will become optional
for our species."

114
00:05:16,857 --> 00:05:21,381
The respectful and ethical minds "need
to stay distinctly human." Not obsolete.

115
00:05:21,421 --> 00:05:23,010
Not its opposite. Optional.

116
00:05:23,156 --> 00:05:26,686
Rooms you no longer have to enter,
but are still allowed to.

117
00:05:26,767 --> 00:05:31,237
So that's the house. Six lights on,
one door ajar, one room dark on purpose.

118
00:05:31,546 --> 00:05:34,352
If you think room seven is next,
tell me in the comments.

119
00:05:34,392 --> 00:05:36,167
What would a machine
actually have to show you,

120
00:05:36,187 --> 00:05:39,834
in a conversation with you,
before you'd switch that light on?

121
00:05:40,192 --> 00:05:41,603
Eight rooms, six lights.

122
00:05:41,782 --> 00:05:45,212
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for
Humanitarians AI, signing off.
